--TEST--
`Core\Socket::upgrade` and `Core\Sse::upgrade` are two doors onto one isolate, so each names the hand-over it is missing rather than one report standing for both — ADR 0083 §§ 1 and 5
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0083 § 5's decision is that these are two cells on the request carrier
// and not one: a WebSocket upgrade *takes the socket*, and only a request the
// server framed an upgrade for has one to give, while an event stream takes
// nothing and is offered to every request a server answers. A single slot
// carrying both hand-overs would be a slot the connection has to ask the kind
// of, and the observable half of that refusal is here -- two members, two
// reports, neither of which is the other's.
//
// This is asserted from a command-line script, where *both* are missing, which
// is what makes the two reports comparable: the only thing that can differ is
// what each member asked for.
try {
    Core\Socket::upgrade("sockets/chat.nvs");
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Sse::upgrade("streams/feed.nvs");
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// And the two are one model behind those doors -- § 5's "the same model
// without `receive`" -- so what a program writes at either is the same clause:
// the same operand, the same optional `args:`, and the same `void`. Counting
// the refusals is how that is said without asserting a third copy of either
// message: the clause's other shape, written once at each door, and both of
// them a throw rather than a `void` one of the two answered.
var $refusals = 0;
try {
    Core\Socket::upgrade("sockets/chat.nvs", args: {room: "lobby"});
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    $refusals = $refusals + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Sse::upgrade("streams/feed.nvs", args: {room: "lobby"});
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    $refusals = $refusals + 1;
}
echo $refusals, "\n";
--EXPECT--
`Core\Socket::upgrade` needs a connection to upgrade and this request arrived on none: only a request a server framed an upgrade for is offered one
`Core\Sse::upgrade` needs a request a server is answering and this is not one: every request the server runs is offered an event stream, and a program run from the command line, a `spawn script` child and a `#[Test]` method are not requests
2
